---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Plan from '../components/Plan.astro';
import { company, checks, plan, proof, numbers } from '../data/product';

const lead = proof[0];
const blocking = checks.filter((check) => check.severity === 'block');
---

<Base title="Plumbline — the review step in front of your migrations" description={company.positioning}>
  <section class="section-tight grid">
    <div class="c-1-7">
      <p class="label">Database change management</p>
      <h1 class="display" style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">Your migration tool will run anything you give it.</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">{company.positioning}</p>
      <div class="actions" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
        <a class="ask" href="/start">Start a {company.trial.days}-day trial</a>
        <a class="ask-quiet" href="/checks">See what it refuses</a>
      </div>
      <p class="actions-note" style="margin-top: var(--s-2);">
        {company.trial.scope} · no card · {company.people} people in Manchester
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-9-4 end">
      {/* Reviewed as too quiet for what it carries. It is the caption to the figure that
          makes the whole argument, so it is set as one: a rule, a number, and a sentence
          at reading size rather than at footnote size. */}
      <div class="figure-lead">
        <p class="label">The figure below</p>
        <p class="figure-lead-count"><span>3</span> of 6</p>
        <p class="figure-lead-text">
          statements in this migration plan would have reached production. It is Plumbline's
          own output, rendered as live HTML rather than photographed as a screenshot.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* The signature moment, and it is placed before anything else has been claimed: the
      product's own output, at full width, in the first screen and a half. A category full
      of tools promising to catch problems is a category where showing beats telling. */}
  <section class="grid">
    <div class="c-full">
      <Plan />
      <p class="label" style="margin-top: var(--s-2);">
        Bars are drawn against this team's stated {plan.budgetMs}ms lock budget — the vertical rule — not against the worst statement in the plan.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section grid">
    <div class="c-full">
      <div class="numbers">
        {numbers.map((entry) => (
          <div>
            <span class="number-figure">{entry.figure}</span>
            <span class="number-caption">{entry.caption}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="label" style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">
        Plumbline is a fictional company built as a design prototype. These figures and the
        quotation below are invented, and are here to show how such evidence would be set —
        not to be believed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight grid rule-top">
    <div class="c-1-6">
      <h2 class="h2">It is the third one that gets you.</h2>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">
        Every statement in that plan is one a competent engineer wrote and a competent
        reviewer approved. None of them is wrong. Three of them are wrong <em>against twelve
        million rows, during a rolling deploy, at half past four on a Friday</em> — and that
        context is not in the diff, which is why review misses it.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Plumbline has the context. It reads the target's live table statistics, the query
        logs for the last seven days, and the set of migrations the deploy will apply
        together. Then it says no, with the arithmetic attached.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top: var(--s-4);"><a class="ask-text" href="/checks">All eight checks, and how each one is detected</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="c-8-5">
      <p class="label">The five it blocks outright</p>
      <ul style="list-style: none; margin: var(--s-3) 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--s-2);">
        {blocking.map((check) => (
          <li style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 4rem 1fr; gap: var(--s-3); padding-bottom: var(--s-2); border-bottom: var(--hair);">
            <span class="check-id">{check.id}</span>
            <span style="font-size: var(--t-sm);">{check.title}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section grid rule-top">
    <div class="c-1-7">
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>“{lead.quote}”</p>
        <footer class="label">{lead.who} · {lead.org}</footer>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
    <div class="c-9-4">
      <p class="label">What it does not do</p>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-top: var(--s-2); font-size: var(--t-sm);">
        Plumbline never connects to your database to change it, and it never runs a
        statement. It reads, it plans, and it returns an exit code. Your existing migration
        tool does the work, or does not.
      </p>
      <p><a class="ask-text" href="/security">How the connection works</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight grid rule-top">
    <div class="c-1-8">
      <h2 class="h2">Put it in front of one database and see what it says.</h2>
      <p class="prose" style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">
        The trial runs against a single database for {company.trial.days} days. It is read-only,
        it needs no card, and if it never refuses anything you will have lost an afternoon.
      </p>
      <div class="actions" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
        <a class="ask" href="/start">Start a {company.trial.days}-day trial</a>
        <a class="ask-quiet" href="/pricing">See pricing</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
